                  account for the main scientific observations regarding the
                  elementary mechanisms of conscious processing in the human
                  brain. The GNW hypothesis proposes that, in the conscious
                  state, a non-linear network ignition associated with recurrent
                  processing amplifies and sustains a neural representation,
                  allowing the corresponding information to be globally accessed
                  by local processors. We examine this hypothesis in light of
                  recent data that contrast brain activity evoked by either
                  conscious or non-conscious contents, as well as during
                  conscious or non-conscious states, particularly general
                  anesthesia. We also discuss the relationship between the
                  intertwined concepts of conscious processing, attention, and
                  working memory.},
  note         = {- Access consciousness (information accessible to verbal
                  report) vs phenomenal consciousness (pure subjective
                  experience) - Ignition: the sudden coherent and exclusive
                  activation of a subset of workspace neurons coding for the
                  current conscious content - On seen trials vs unseen trials,
                  stimuli evoke a series of metastable activity states and
                  dynamics contain detailed decodable information about the
                  specific stimulus - Anesthesia may disrupt long-range
                  connectivity that functions as a broadcast of information into
