  abstract  = {We propose an efficient design of Transformer-based models for
               multivariate time series forecasting and self-supervised
               representation learning. It is based on two key components: (i)
               segmentation of time series into subseries-level patches which
               are served as input tokens to Transformer; (ii)
               channel-independence where each channel contains a single
               univariate time series that shares the same embedding and
               Transformer weights across all the series. Patching design
               naturally has three-fold benefit: local semantic information is
               retained in the embedding; computation and memory usage of the
               attention maps are quadratically reduced given the same look-back
               window; and the model can attend longer history. Our
               channel-independent patch time series Transformer (PatchTST) can
               improve the long-term forecasting accuracy significantly when
               compared with that of SOTA Transformer-based models. We also
               apply our model to self-supervised pre-training tasks and attain
               excellent fine-tuning performance, which outperforms supervised
               training on large datasets. Transferring of masked pre-training
