  journaltitle = {bioRxiv},
  pages        = {2021.04.15.439917},
  date         = {2021-04-16},
  doi          = {10.1101/2021.04.15.439917},
  abstract     = {The convergent evolution of the fly and mouse olfactory system
                  led us to ask whether the anatomic connectivity and functional
                  logic in vivo would evolve in artificial neural networks
                  constructed to perform olfactory tasks. Artificial networks
                  trained to classify odor identity recapitulate the
                  connectivity inherent in the olfactory system. Input units are
                  driven by a single receptor type, and units driven by the same
                  receptor converge to form a glomerulus. Glomeruli exhibit
                  sparse, unstructured connectivity to a larger, expansion
                  layer. When trained to both classify odor and impart innate
                  valence on odors, the network develops independent pathways
                  for innate output and odor classification. Thus, artificial
                  networks evolve even without the biological mechanisms
                  necessary to build these systems in vivo , providing a
                  rationale for the convergent evolution of olfactory circuits.
