  pages        = {1072--1079},
  date         = {2009-12},
  doi          = {10.1016/j.janxdis.2009.07.009},
  abstract     = {The present study investigated how trait anxiety alters the
                  balance between attentional control systems to impact
                  performance of a discrete preplanned goal-directed motor task.
                  Participants executed targeted force contractions (engaging
                  the goal-directed attentional system) at the offset of
                  emotional and non-emotional distractors (engaging the
                  stimulus-driven attentional system). High and low anxious
                  participants completed the protocol at two target force levels
                  (10\% and 35\% of maximum voluntary contraction). Reaction
                  time (RT), performance accuracy, and rate of change of force
                  were calculated. Expectations were confirmed at the 10\% but
                  not the 35\% target force level: (1) high anxiety was
                  associated with slower RTs, and (2) threat cues lead to faster
