  doi          = {10.1016/j.cub.2013.07.075},
  abstract     = {Neuronal theories of conscious access tentatively relate
                  conscious perception to the integration and global
                  broadcasting of information across distant cortical and
                  thalamic areas. Experiments contrasting visible and invisible
                  stimuli support this view and suggest that global neuronal
                  communication may be detectable using scalp
                  electroencephalography (EEG). However, whether global
                  information sharing across brain areas also provides a
                  specific signature of conscious state in awake but
                  noncommunicating patients remains an active topic of research.
                  We designed a novel measure termed "weighted symbolic mutual
                  information" (wSMI) and applied it to 181 high-density EEG
                  recordings of awake patients recovering from coma and
                  diagnosed in various states of consciousness. The results
                  demonstrate that this measure of information sharing
                  systematically increases with consciousness state,
                  particularly across distant sites. This effect sharply
                  distinguishes patients in vegetative state (VS), minimally
                  conscious state (MCS), and conscious state (CS) and is
                  observed regardless of etiology and delay since insult. The
                  present findings support distributed theories of conscious
                  processing and open up the possibility of an automatic
                  detection of conscious states, which may be particularly
