  date         = {2022-12-23},
  doi          = {10.1126/science.abq6740},
  abstract     = {Learning to predict rewards based on environmental cues is
                  essential for survival. It is believed that animals learn to
                  predict rewards by updating predictions whenever the outcome
                  deviates from expectations, and that such reward prediction
                  errors (RPEs) are signaled by the mesolimbic dopamine system-a
                  key controller of learning. However, instead of learning
                  prospective predictions from RPEs, animals can infer
                  predictions by learning the retrospective cause of rewards.
                  Hence, whether mesolimbic dopamine instead conveys a causal
                  associative signal that sometimes resembles RPE remains
                  unknown. We developed an algorithm for retrospective causal
                  learning and found that mesolimbic dopamine release conveys
