                  mechanisms. By contrast, RNNs can also be trained on the
                  measured physiological and behavioural data, thereby directly
                  inheriting their temporal and geometrical properties. In this
                  way they become a formal surrogate for the experimentally
                  probed system that can be further analysed, perturbed and
                  simulated. This powerful approach is called dynamical system
                  reconstruction. In this Perspective, we focus on recent trends
                  in artificial intelligence and machine learning in this
                  exciting and rapidly expanding field, which may be less well
                  known in neuroscience. We discuss formal prerequisites,
                  different model architectures and training approaches for
                  RNN-based dynamical system reconstructions, ways to evaluate
