                  delivered in each trial regardless of their behavioral
                  response. Ketamine was infused for 60 min. Results
                  Ketamine-induced loss of consciousness was identified during a
                  gradual evolution of the high beta-gamma oscillations. The
                  slow oscillations appeared to develop at a later stage of
                  ketamine anesthesia. Return of consciousness and return of
                  preanesthetic performance level (performance return) were
                  observed during a gradual drift of the gamma oscillations
                  toward the beta frequency. Ketamine-induced loss of
                  consciousness, return of consciousness, and performance return
                  are all identified during a gradual change of the dynamics,
