                  ketamine and delayed in recovery. Conclusions Ketamine
                  generates unique intracortical dynamics during its altered
                  states of consciousness, suggesting fundamentally different
                  neuronal processes from propofol. The gradually shifting
                  dynamics suggest a continuously conscious or dreaming state
                  while unresponsive under ketamine until its deeper stage with
                  the slow-delta oscillations. Somatosensory processing is
                  preserved during ketamine anesthesia, but multisensory
                  processing appears to be diminished under ketamine and through
                  recovery. Editor’s Perspective What We Already Know about This
                  Topic What This Article Tells Us That Is New},
